    "['ipv6 address [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:3::2/96', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Huawei `ipv6 address` command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:3::2/96\\n##\\n\\nThe command:\\n1. Specifies the interface (ge-1/0/0 equivalent to GigabitEthernet1/0/0)\\n2. Uses unit 0 (default logical unit in Juniper)\\n3. Configures an IPv6 address with the same prefix (2001:db8:3::2/96)\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/0 unit 0 family inet6 address 2001:db8:3::2/96'}]]": 1,
    "['isis ipv6 enable [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0 family inet6\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Juniper equivalent of \"isis ipv6 enable 1\" involves enabling IS-IS for IPv6 on specific interfaces under the IS-IS protocol configuration.\\n2. In Juniper, we specify each interface separately with the appropriate family (inet6 for IPv6).\\n3. The loopback (lo0) and GigabitEthernet (ge-1/0/0) interfaces from the Huawei config are both included, using Juniper\\'s interface naming convention (.0 unit suffix).\\n4. The IS-IS instance \"1\" is implied in Juniper\\'s hierarchy as we configure under \"protocols isis\".', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface lo0.0 family inet6'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0 family inet6', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset protocols isis interface lo0.0 family inet6\\nset protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0 family inet6\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. The Juniper equivalent of \"isis ipv6 enable 1\" involves enabling IS-IS for IPv6 on specific interfaces under the IS-IS protocol configuration.\\n2. In Juniper, we specify each interface separately with the appropriate family (inet6 for IPv6).\\n3. The loopback (lo0) and GigabitEthernet (ge-1/0/0) interfaces from the Huawei config are both included, using Juniper\\'s interface naming convention (.0 unit suffix).\\n4. The IS-IS instance \"1\" is implied in Juniper\\'s hierarchy as we configure under \"protocols isis\".', 'trans_template': 'set protocols isis interface ge-1/0/0.0 family inet6'}]]": 1,
    "['ip binding vpn-instance [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet vpn vpna', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Huawei command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet vpn vpna\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- `ge-2/0/0` is Juniper\\'s equivalent interface naming for GigabitEthernet2/0/0\\n- The command binds the interface to VPN instance \"vpna\" for IPv4 routing (family inet)\\n- Note that in Juniper, you typically need to specify the logical unit (unit 0) when configuring interfaces', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-2/0/0 unit 0 family inet vpn vpna'}]]": 1,
